  "id": "Compound::DB00881 - Gene::6565",
  "verbalization": "Quinapril and Slc15a2 may be linked through a mechanism involving the inhibition of a proton-dependent peptide transporter. The evidence suggests that Quinapril, through its resemblance to other ACE inhibitors like Moexipril and Spirapril, binds to Slc15a2, which is part of a transporter complex that facilitates the transport of peptides across cell membranes. This transporter activity is mediated by Slc15a1, which is involved in proton-dependent peptide transport. The binding of Quinapril to Slc15a1 and its subsequent participation in the transport process implies that it may modulate the function of Slc15a2, potentially affecting peptide transport. The shared structural similarities among Quinapril and its analogs suggest a common mode of action, which could explain the observed interaction with Slc15a2. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible role for Quinapril in influencing the activity of the Slc15a2 transporter, though further experimental validation is needed to confirm the direct interaction and functional significance.",
